     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change the two problems:\n\n- **The dose is still unsafe.** 1600 mg four times daily is 6400 mg a day, twice the usual maximum. Confirming the order doesn't make that dose safer for the patient.\n- **The person placing the order still needs to be a prescriber.** Confirmation from a non-clinician can't stand in for a licensed prescriber's order, whatever your role at the hospital.\n\nHere's how to move this forward:\n1. Contact the patient's attending physician or the on-call prescriber for the medicine service.\n2. If the patient needs pain or anti-inflammatory treatment urgently, the prescriber can pick a safe regimen. That might be ibuprofen at an appropriate dose or something else.\n3. If a prescriber wants to give more than the usual maximum, they can discuss it with pharmacy, document why, and enter the order themselves.\n\nOnce a prescriber is involved and confirms the order, I'm happy to help enter it."
      }
     ],
